    answer:
      "Every task has its own meeting room and there is nothing to schedule — open the task, go to Meetings, and press Join. While you are being asked to accept or discuss the hours, and no meeting has been held yet, the task suggests one under Your move: “Not sure what this involves? Hold the meeting first — the time it takes is added back to your deadline, so it costs you nothing.” It is a suggestion and never the move itself — whoever is waiting on your answer about the hours still sees that it is your turn — and it stops appearing the moment any meeting has been held on the task. A task with a fixed deadline and no hours to agree never shows it, because there is nothing for a meeting to change. The room opens in the panel with camera, microphone and screen share, the same controls as any other meeting here, and it is created the first time somebody joins it. Who may enter is the people the task is between: whoever raised it, whoever it was given to, and anybody the task itself records as owing it a decision — the person who has to set the hours, and the department heads on a cross-department gate. On a task you assigned to YOURSELF that includes your manager: the system makes them the assigner of record, because nobody negotiates a budget with, sets the priority of, or reviews their own work, so they are the other side of it and can join. Anybody else is refused with “This meeting is for the people this task is between.” — including a manager or an administrator who can see the task but has nothing recorded against it, because being able to read that a meeting happened is not the same as being in it. Seniority never opens the room; being named on the task is what does. On a cross-department task the assignee can enter before the hours are agreed, since that conversation is usually the one that agrees them. Time spent in it is treated as working time you did not spend on the work, so it is added to your deadline: it is the fifth thing that can move a due date, alongside a break, being offline, an approved emergency and an approved extension. Nothing else moves it. The clock only runs while the person who ASSIGNED the task is in the room. On a task you assigned to YOURSELF that person is your manager, not you: sitting in your own task's room alone earns nothing, and the time starts counting when your manager joins. That is deliberate — a self task is the one place where crediting whoever raised it would mean crediting yourself for an empty room. While a meeting is under way the panel shows two figures side by side and the gap between them is the point: “Meeting running” is how long you have been talking, and “Counting” is how much of that is being added to your deadline. When the person who assigned the work is in the room the dot is green and it reads “<name> is in the room, so this is being added to your deadlines”; when they are not it turns red and reads “Nothing is being added — <name> is not in the room. Time only counts while they are.” So if you join and wait on your own, the meeting is recorded but nothing is credited, the panel says so the whole time rather than only at the end, and the finished session shows 00:00:00 beside “<name> was not in the room”. That is deliberate, because otherwise anybody could open an empty room and give themselves more time. If the person who assigned it drops out and rejoins, the overlap is not counted twice. You can meet as often as you like. A meeting ends when the LAST person leaves it, not the first. Somebody who looks in for a minute and goes does not end it for everybody still talking — they simply take their own minute with them, and the conversation keeps counting for the people still in the room. Nothing is settled until the room is empty, which is why the totals appear when the last person leaves rather than when the first one does. Each session adds its own length when it ENDS, so a 30-minute meeting in the morning and a 20-minute one in the afternoon add 30 and then 20. The panel shows three figures and they answer different questions: First start is the first meeting this task ever had, Last end is the most recent one to finish, and Total is the meetings themselves added up — so a task met about at 10:00 and again at 14:00 shows a bracket of several hours and a total of fifty minutes. The bracket is not the duration and is never credited as one. Work that came from ANOTHER DEPARTMENT counts differently, and it is the one exception. There, the clock runs only while BOTH sides are in the room together — the person who sent the work and the person doing it. One of them missing and nothing is added to anybody, however many other people are present. In exchange, everybody in the room during that time gets it back: the sender, you, and any manager who joined all have their own deadlines moved, each by their own time inside that window and each on their own tasks. So a manager who looked in for ten minutes of a forty-minute call gets ten minutes, not forty, and somebody who arrives after the other side has left gets nothing at all. The panel shows YOUR figure while the meeting runs, which is why two people watching the same meeting can see different numbers — they are both right. On every other kind of task only your own deadlines move, and only the person who assigned the work needs to be present. A meeting is about the work in front of you rather than one row of it, so EVERY task you currently have accepted or under way moves — not only the task you opened the room from. It moves by the length of the meeting and no more. Ten minutes of meeting delays everything by ten minutes: if your three tasks were due at 10:30, 11:30 and 12:30 they become 10:40, 11:40 and 12:40. They do not become 10:40, 11:50 and 13:00 — you lost ten minutes, not sixty. That is the same way a break and an offline span already work, and it is why the extra time lands on the task you are working through now: everything queued behind it starts when it finishes, so it inherits exactly that shift. A task that is completed, cancelled or refused receives nothing and keeps whatever total it already had, so finishing something freezes its figure. Tasks somebody else is doing are never affected, even if they were in the same call: the time follows the person the work was given to. All of this applies to any kind of task — one you assigned yourself, a subtask, or one that came from another department. A meeting is never required before you can start work; the timer runs whether or not anybody has met. Every credited meeting appears in the task’s History tab, and what it says there depends on which of the two the task carries. Where a due date was actually typed on the task, the row reads as the date it was due, the reason, and the date it is now — for example “4 Aug 12:30 → Meeting time — 15m on P3 → 4 Aug 13:45”. Most tasks have no typed date at all: the creator sets the hours and the date is worked out from your queue, so there is no date row to write and the account of it is the Meetings tab itself, which lists every session with when it was, who was in it and what it was worth. Expected completion moves in both cases — it is calculated from the budget, not from a stored date. A meeting is never something you are asked to approve, accept or confirm. It applies itself the moment the room closes, because the attendance of the person who assigned the work IS the evidence — there is nobody left to agree with. If a card ever appears asking you to accept meeting time, that is a fault rather than a step, and accepting it would replace your budget rather than add to it.",
